 ## Background
-The Texture Buffer Object (TBO) technique was introduced in OpenGL 3.1 [S1, unofficial]. It allows buffer objects to be accessed as textures, providing a way to use texture sampling functions for buffer data. This is particularly useful for volume rendering where voxel data (e.g., block types) needs to be looked up efficiently [S1, unofficial]. The method is often compared to SSBO, which was introduced in OpenGL 4.3 and offers more flexibility but requires newer hardware [S1, unofficial].
+The Texture Buffer Object (TBO) technique was introduced in OpenGL 3.1 [S1, unofficial]. It allows buffer objects to be accessed as textures, providing a way to use texture sampling functions for buffer data [S1, unofficial]. This is particularly useful for volume rendering where voxel data (e.g., block types) needs to be looked up efficiently [S1, unofficial]. The method is presented as an alternative to SSBO for older hardware, as SSBO requires OpenGL 4.3 or higher [S1, unofficial].
 
